2. Webhook endpoint (core of the demo)
This is the code you show.

If Node.js:

app.post('/webhook', express.raw({type: 'application/json'}), (req, res) => {
  const event = stripe.webhooks.constructEvent(req.body, sig, endpointSecret);
  if (event.type === 'payment_intent.succeeded') {
    // 1. log to DB / Sheets
    // 2. send receipt email
  }
  if (event.type === 'charge.refunded') { /* update sheet row to REFUNDED */ }
  if (event.type === 'payment_intent.payment_failed') { /* log FAILED + reason */ }
  res.json({received: true});
});

If Python (Flask):
Same logic — stripe.Webhook.construct_event(), then branch on event type.


3. Google Sheets logging (makes it visual for non-technical clients)

Create a Sheet with columns: Date | Email | Amount | Status | Stripe ID
Use Google Sheets API + service account
When payment_intent.succeeded -> append row
When refunded -> find row by Stripe ID and update Status to REFUNDED
If Sheets API is annoying, log to SQLite/Postgres first and say "can sync to Sheets / QuickBooks" — still counts.


4. Email receipt (2 lines, huge trust boost)
Use Resend / SendGrid in test mode. Subject: "Receipt for $29.00" — clients love seeing you thought about their customer.

